From a0a0f22e17eb2d9a44e0e911d8bd554da9a3093d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:11:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] relay_metrics: Count BEGIN_DIR traffic by kind of previous
 hop

Under a BEGIN_DIR flood, the first question is whether the streams come
from tor clients over one-hop circuits (an unauthenticated previous
hop: ordinary directory fetches) or over circuits extended by relays
(an authenticated previous hop: onion service descriptor requests),
because the two call for different fixes. The metrics answered it only
for onion service requests that were refused as single hop; other
directory requests, streams that never sent a request, and CREATE cells
carried no previous hop at all.

Classify a circuit's previous hop as "client" (the channel never
authenticated: a tor client or a bridge), "relay" (an authenticated
relay in our consensus, or whose descriptor we have), or
"unknown_relay" (an authenticated relay identity we don't know, which
passes the single-hop check). Count CREATE cells by that kind in
dos_cc_new_create_cell(), and BEGIN_DIR cells and the circuits that
send them in dos_should_refuse_begindir(), which notes the kind on the
circuit once; its directory streams copy it, so a stream that closes
before its request arrives is still attributed. Add the kind as label
prev_hop to tor_relay_dir_requests_total, and new families
tor_relay_create_cells_total, tor_relay_begindir_cells_total and
tor_relay_begindir_circuits_total.

An onion service request that fails connection_dir_is_anonymous() is
now counted as "single_hop" only if its previous hop never
authenticated, and as the new action "closing" if its stream or circuit
was already closing, which used to inflate single_hop when a client or
a defense closed a circuit with requests still queued.

The counters live in dos.c beside the hooks it already has, so no new
include reaches connection_edge.c or command.c. The cost is one
known-relay lookup per CREATE cell and per circuit's first BEGIN_DIR
cell, and three bits per circuit and two per directory connection.

Part of #41339.
